From 60d410c51803d5a5ac00d8e023bb2167569b09fb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: terminills <tdeters@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 20:43:26 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 631/638] aros: stop citing the trace-contaminated frame-time
 runs as evidence

The --aros-multi-process comments said frame-time measurements showed the
renderer main thread to be the bottleneck. Those runs were dominated by
the per-decommit serial trace turned off in 67b78a322e (fps 3.5 -> 12.0
on the same guest with nothing else changed), so they do not show where
the bottleneck is. The structural argument - one renderer main thread
per single-process browser - stands on its own.

---
 base/base_switches.cc       | 5 ++---
 content/app/content_main.cc | 9 +++++----
 2 files changed, 7 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

diff --git a/base/base_switches.cc b/base/base_switches.cc
index 9d1ac431d8..cdc8cb9932 100644
--- a/base/base_switches.cc
+++ b/base/base_switches.cc
@@ -243,9 +243,8 @@ const char kArosNativeThreadPool[] = "aros-native-threadpool";
 // processes.
 //
 // This is the largest available multicore lever and the one that was being
-// missed: single-process means every tab shares ONE renderer main thread, and
-// frame-time measurements show the main thread - not the thread pool - is the
-// bottleneck. Per-tab parallelism is zero until this is on. Pairs with
+// missed: single-process means every tab shares ONE renderer main thread.
+// Per-tab parallelism is zero until this is on. Pairs with
 // per-process placement in base/process/launch_aros.cc, since a child process
 // inherits its launcher's core exactly as a thread does.
 //
diff --git a/content/app/content_main.cc b/content/app/content_main.cc
index 3acb945f73..103a0c1a67 100644
--- a/content/app/content_main.cc
+++ b/content/app/content_main.cc
@@ -155,10 +155,11 @@ void ApplyArosProcessModelDefaults(base::CommandLine* command_line) {
   // switch there was no way to ask for the multi-process model at all.
   //
   // This matters more than any thread-pool tuning. Single-process means every
-  // tab shares one renderer main thread, and the main thread is what the
-  // frame-time measurements show to be the bottleneck - so per-tab parallelism
-  // is currently exactly zero, and no scheduling policy inside the one process
-  // can change that.
+  // tab shares one renderer main thread, so per-tab parallelism is currently
+  // exactly zero, and no scheduling policy inside the one process can change
+  // that. (An earlier version of this comment cited frame-time measurements
+  // for the main thread being the bottleneck; those runs were dominated by a
+  // per-decommit serial trace, since turned off, and are not evidence.)
   //
   // Left OFF by default: multi-process on AROS is untested, and the sandbox and
   // GPU-process defaults below stay as they are, so this changes one axis at a
